WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.482 --> 00:00:01.991
- You're a success every day.

00:00:01.991 --> 00:00:05.141
Success is a daily thing,
not a destination thing.

00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:08.758
If you can believe it, you can achieve it.

00:00:08.758 --> 00:00:11.959
And that never quite rang true with me.

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:15.032
This something for nothing
entitlement mindset

00:00:15.032 --> 00:00:18.291
that invades so many people's
lives is a bunch of garbage.

00:00:18.291 --> 00:00:20.137
Too many leaders, they're
like travel agents.

00:00:20.137 --> 00:00:23.794
They're sending people where
they've never been themselves.

00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:24.627
And you want to be a tour guide.

00:00:24.627 --> 00:00:26.496
You want to take people with you.

00:00:26.496 --> 00:00:29.117
- He's an American
author, speaker and pastor

00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:30.524
who's written many books.

00:00:30.524 --> 00:00:32.711
His books have sold millions
of copies worldwide,

00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:35.482
with some making the New
York Times bestseller lists.

00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:38.179
Ink Magazine named him
the number one leadership

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:40.603
and management expert in the world.

00:00:40.603 --> 00:00:41.436
He is John Maxwell,

00:00:41.436 --> 00:00:44.188
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:44.188 --> 00:00:45.935
Rule number eight is my personal favorite,

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:47.607
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:50.024
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:52.816 --> 00:00:53.682
- The first thing I want you to know,

00:00:53.682 --> 00:00:57.849
that success is not a
destination, it's a journey.

00:00:59.326 --> 00:01:02.089
Think of success as a process.

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:05.839
Let me illustrate it
and explain it this way.

00:01:07.124 --> 00:01:09.687
If you go to college, you work hard,

00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:10.927
and in four or five years,

00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:13.502
depending on what kind of
degree you're working on,

00:01:13.502 --> 00:01:15.762
and in today's society
sometimes six or seven years,

00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:19.361
but eventually it comes
the day of graduation,

00:01:19.361 --> 00:01:20.194
and you're all excited,

00:01:20.194 --> 00:01:23.358
and your family is there
and your friends are there,

00:01:23.358 --> 00:01:24.785
and you're there with your classmates,

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:26.658
you've got your cap and your gown,

00:01:26.658 --> 00:01:28.280
and you know that there's
going to be a time

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.127
in that ceremony where you're going

00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:33.239
to walk across stage and
the president, provost,

00:01:33.239 --> 00:01:34.294
somebody's going to shake your hand,

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:36.378
hand you the diploma, congratulate you,

00:01:36.378 --> 00:01:37.869
and you're going to
get off the other side,

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:39.269
they're going to have
presents waiting for you,

00:01:39.269 --> 00:01:40.306
and they'll be taking pictures,

00:01:40.306 --> 00:01:41.716
and everybody will be shaking your hand,

00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:43.049
saying, "congratulations.

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:46.218
"Today you become a success,
you're a college graduate."

00:01:46.218 --> 00:01:47.468
Now, my friend,

00:01:49.488 --> 00:01:52.622
you did not become a success

00:01:52.622 --> 00:01:55.663
the day that you got your diploma.

00:01:55.663 --> 00:01:57.952
Now, what you did have happen
to you in that ceremony

00:01:57.952 --> 00:02:00.785
is you got recognized for success.

00:02:02.418 --> 00:02:06.893
The diploma is recognition
of what you have done

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:08.438
the previous four or five years.

00:02:08.438 --> 00:02:11.495
You see, you were a success
in your freshman year

00:02:11.495 --> 00:02:13.600
when you decided to not drop out of school

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.223
like some of your other classmates,

00:02:15.223 --> 00:02:17.009
and decided to stick to it.

00:02:17.009 --> 00:02:20.160
And you were a success every
time you studied for a test.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.440
And you were a success every
time you did a project,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.420
or did a writing assignment.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.955
You see, you're a success
all through school.

00:02:27.955 --> 00:02:29.596
You're a success every day.

00:02:29.596 --> 00:02:33.117
Success is a daily thing,
not a destination thing.

00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:36.377
The day you got the diploma
you just got recognized

00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:39.544
for the success that you already were.

00:02:41.346 --> 00:02:43.708
Now, that's very essential.

00:02:43.708 --> 00:02:48.208
Because so many times,
people have a tendency

00:02:48.208 --> 00:02:50.625
to devalue the moment, today.

00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:57.506
What they do is they greatly
value the destination.

00:02:57.506 --> 00:02:59.137
And so they kind of talk about,

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.607
when I get there or if I arrive there,

00:03:01.607 --> 00:03:04.998
or when I do that or
when I accomplish this.

00:03:04.998 --> 00:03:08.957
And they don't understand
that success is a daily thing.

00:03:08.957 --> 00:03:10.896
And I'm here to share with you

00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:14.199
that the secret of success is determined

00:03:14.199 --> 00:03:15.949
by your daily agenda.

00:03:17.972 --> 00:03:19.731
In fact, I wrote a book a few years ago

00:03:19.731 --> 00:03:21.710
called Today Matters.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:22.831
I'm passionate about that book,

00:03:22.831 --> 00:03:24.700
because what it does is it helps you,

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:29.302
it helps me to understand
that we make decisions

00:03:29.302 --> 00:03:31.838
and then we manage decisions.

00:03:31.838 --> 00:03:34.832
And too often we think,
I will make a decision,

00:03:34.832 --> 00:03:35.734
for example, you're saying,

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:37.727
"I'm going to make a
decision to be a coach."

00:03:37.727 --> 00:03:42.053
Or, "I'm going to make a
decision to be a public speaker.

00:03:42.053 --> 00:03:43.124
"I want to be a communicator."

00:03:43.124 --> 00:03:45.078
Well, congratulations.

00:03:45.078 --> 00:03:46.777
You've made a wonderful decision.

00:03:46.777 --> 00:03:49.444
Coach, speaking, good decisions.

00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:52.964
But that won't make
you a successful coach.

00:03:52.964 --> 00:03:56.176
That won't make you a
successful communicator.

00:03:56.176 --> 00:03:59.554
It's not the decision that makes you.

00:03:59.554 --> 00:04:03.827
You've got to make the
decision by managing it.

00:04:03.827 --> 00:04:06.797
And you manage the
decision on a daily basis.

00:04:06.797 --> 00:04:10.931
In other words, what
you want to be tomorrow,

00:04:10.931 --> 00:04:12.848
you've got to do today.

00:04:15.443 --> 00:04:17.360
You visualize tomorrow.

00:04:18.979 --> 00:04:20.142
That gives you hope.

00:04:20.142 --> 00:04:22.600
That's your motivation,
and that's your dream.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:23.701
Nothing wrong with that.

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:27.368
You visualize tomorrow,
but you value today.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.008
What's that mean?

00:04:32.008 --> 00:04:35.566
That means that what I do every day

00:04:35.566 --> 00:04:38.257
is either getting me
closer to that vision,

00:04:38.257 --> 00:04:40.090
that dream, that goal,

00:04:40.941 --> 00:04:45.676
or it's really driving
me farther away from it.

00:04:45.676 --> 00:04:49.259
You see, every day, we
are either repairing

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.377
or we're preparing.

00:04:52.377 --> 00:04:54.170
You see, if I messed up yesterday,

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.768
guess what I get to do today?

00:04:55.768 --> 00:04:57.429
Fix yesterday. (laughs)

00:04:57.429 --> 00:05:00.280
In other words, if I didn't
do the right thing yesterday,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.658
what I got to do today
is I've got to repair.

00:05:02.658 --> 00:05:04.415
I've got to go back, make amends,

00:05:04.415 --> 00:05:07.335
backtrack, put the car in
reverse, put my life in reverse.

00:05:07.335 --> 00:05:08.581
I've got to go back there.

00:05:08.581 --> 00:05:09.631
I've got to repair.

00:05:09.631 --> 00:05:12.165
Now, every day I spend repairing,

00:05:12.165 --> 00:05:14.330
I'm not spending preparing.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.996
Well, you see, we repair
when we fail to manage

00:05:17.996 --> 00:05:20.235
the decisions that we've made.

00:05:20.235 --> 00:05:23.600
We prepare when we, on a daily basis,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.647
manage the decisions that we've made.

00:05:25.647 --> 00:05:28.064
So your footprints to success

00:05:29.332 --> 00:05:32.082
are really footprints of success.

00:05:33.542 --> 00:05:37.375
Because every step that is made and taken,

00:05:37.375 --> 00:05:41.424
based upon the goals that
you have for your life,

00:05:41.424 --> 00:05:43.417
and you manage those goals correctly,

00:05:43.417 --> 00:05:46.781
every step is the progressive realization

00:05:46.781 --> 00:05:49.395
of success in your life.

00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:52.951
And by the way, oh,
you'll get the diploma.

00:05:52.951 --> 00:05:54.974
You'll get the certificate.

00:05:54.974 --> 00:05:58.012
But when you get that, you didn't arrive.

00:05:58.012 --> 00:06:01.535
It just is another step in preparing you

00:06:01.535 --> 00:06:03.535
to reach your potential.

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:06.668
Each one of us should live our life

00:06:06.668 --> 00:06:10.461
as if we'll never learn
everything we need to learn,

00:06:10.461 --> 00:06:12.076
we'll never be able to
accomplish everything

00:06:12.076 --> 00:06:13.385
we wanted to accomplish,

00:06:13.385 --> 00:06:14.813
we won't be able to experience everything

00:06:14.813 --> 00:06:15.751
we wanted to experience.

00:06:15.751 --> 00:06:19.694
We should live our life every day hungry,

00:06:19.694 --> 00:06:23.611
understanding that we
are to live until we die.

00:06:25.788 --> 00:06:29.179
You see, I think success
can't be summarized

00:06:29.179 --> 00:06:32.537
in a flippant degree or program

00:06:32.537 --> 00:06:34.370
or diploma or arrival.

00:06:35.873 --> 00:06:39.597
I think today, if you
are learning to coach,

00:06:39.597 --> 00:06:41.497
if you are learning to speak,

00:06:41.497 --> 00:06:43.843
if you're doing the
things that are essential

00:06:43.843 --> 00:06:46.730
to the decisions, and you're
managing those decisions,

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.201
well, can I say something to you?

00:06:48.201 --> 00:06:49.334
Congratulations.

00:06:49.334 --> 00:06:51.501
You are already a success.

00:06:52.575 --> 00:06:53.908
Now, guess what?

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.877
Over time, it shows up.

00:06:58.945 --> 00:07:00.415
You've heard the expression.

00:07:00.415 --> 00:07:02.697
You maybe even have said it yourself.

00:07:02.697 --> 00:07:04.565
You've heard the expression, I'm sure.

00:07:04.565 --> 00:07:09.478
I've worked all my life to
become an overnight success.

00:07:09.478 --> 00:07:10.774
That's the way it works.

00:07:10.774 --> 00:07:12.828
All of a sudden, somebody recognizes you,

00:07:12.828 --> 00:07:14.883
all of sudden somebody congratulates you.

00:07:14.883 --> 00:07:16.849
You didn't get good at that moment,

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:19.797
you've been good for a long time.

00:07:19.797 --> 00:07:22.047
It just showed up some day.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.139
Have you ever heard a motivational speaker

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:28.729
tell you that attitude is everything?

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:29.562
I bet you have.

00:07:29.562 --> 00:07:31.113
I've heard many say that.

00:07:31.113 --> 00:07:32.639
In fact, they'll say something like,

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:36.373
if you can believe it you can achieve it.

00:07:36.373 --> 00:07:40.403
And that never quite rang true with me.

00:07:40.403 --> 00:07:42.470
So I began to press the issue,

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.082
and I have come to the conclusion

00:07:44.082 --> 00:07:47.487
that attitude isn't everything.

00:07:47.487 --> 00:07:51.034
Now, I'm not disclaiming or in any way

00:07:51.034 --> 00:07:53.011
being disparaging about attitude,

00:07:53.011 --> 00:07:54.870
because I think attitude
is very important.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.287
But if anybody comes
up to you and tells you

00:07:57.287 --> 00:07:59.976
that attitude is everything,
don't buy into that.

00:07:59.976 --> 00:08:00.809
That's not true.

00:08:00.809 --> 00:08:05.144
For example, attitude
can't replace incompetence.

00:08:05.144 --> 00:08:07.149
If you're incompetent,
you can be very happy,

00:08:07.149 --> 00:08:10.514
but you'll still be very unsuccessful.

00:08:10.514 --> 00:08:13.318
Now, here's what I do know.

00:08:13.318 --> 00:08:15.167
Attitude isn't everything,

00:08:15.167 --> 00:08:17.667
but attitude is the main thing

00:08:19.702 --> 00:08:22.235
that'll make a difference in your life.

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:23.154
Because I believe that,

00:08:23.154 --> 00:08:26.804
I wrote the book The Difference Maker.

00:08:26.804 --> 00:08:29.000
You see, that's what attitude is.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.738
Attitude is the difference maker.

00:08:30.738 --> 00:08:32.219
You have two people side by side

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:34.105
that you're thinking about
employing in your company.

00:08:34.105 --> 00:08:35.456
Both of them have the same background,

00:08:35.456 --> 00:08:39.169
the same job description,
education, experience,

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:40.599
I mean, you could almost flip a coin

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:42.105
and say, "I could hire
the one on the right

00:08:42.105 --> 00:08:44.139
"or I could hire the one on the left."

00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:46.752
But one has a great attitude,

00:08:46.752 --> 00:08:47.985
and one has a bad attitude.

00:08:47.985 --> 00:08:50.147
Now, who are you going to hire?

00:08:50.147 --> 00:08:52.183
You're going to hire the
person with the great attitude.

00:08:52.183 --> 00:08:53.484
Why?

00:08:53.484 --> 00:08:56.404
Because it was the difference maker.

00:08:56.404 --> 00:09:00.743
In areas of choice, you need
to work on your weaknesses.

00:09:00.743 --> 00:09:03.493
For example, let's say I am lazy.

00:09:04.365 --> 00:09:06.202
That's an area of choice.

00:09:06.202 --> 00:09:09.558
I'm not naturally lazy, I'm just lazy.

00:09:09.558 --> 00:09:10.587
It's a choice.

00:09:10.587 --> 00:09:12.064
So I need to work on that.

00:09:12.064 --> 00:09:14.248
Because in areas of choices,

00:09:14.248 --> 00:09:17.776
you can make vast improvement
and you can fast improvement.

00:09:17.776 --> 00:09:18.609
- [Interviewer] I've never heard

00:09:18.609 --> 00:09:19.605
this distinction, by the way.

00:09:19.605 --> 00:09:20.522
It's hugely helpful.

00:09:20.522 --> 00:09:22.582
- In choices, attitude.

00:09:22.582 --> 00:09:23.521
Lets say I have a lousy attitude.

00:09:23.521 --> 00:09:25.799
One day I wake up and say this
isn't getting me anywhere.

00:09:25.799 --> 00:09:26.649
I've got to have a good attitude.

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:31.077
You can go from a zero
to a 10 almost overnight.

00:09:31.077 --> 00:09:32.255
You've seen people and you say,

00:09:32.255 --> 00:09:36.198
"my gosh, overnight they
just got good and happy."

00:09:36.198 --> 00:09:37.031
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:37.031 --> 00:09:38.327
That's a choice.

00:09:38.327 --> 00:09:40.744
Vast, fast growth in choices.

00:09:42.255 --> 00:09:43.422
In giftedness,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:45.317
DNA wiring,

00:09:49.179 --> 00:09:51.846
your growth is very, very small.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:54.941
And it's very slow.

00:09:54.941 --> 00:09:56.624
So I think, for example,

00:09:56.624 --> 00:10:00.981
a person can maybe increase
their giftedness skillset

00:10:00.981 --> 00:10:03.236
maybe two numbers.

00:10:03.236 --> 00:10:06.393
So if I'm a little bit
above average, I'm a six,

00:10:06.393 --> 00:10:08.424
if I really work hard
I can become an eight.

00:10:08.424 --> 00:10:11.054
But an eight is powerful.

00:10:11.054 --> 00:10:12.285
Eight has a huge return.

00:10:12.285 --> 00:10:15.651
So what I tell people is
in areas of giftedness,

00:10:15.651 --> 00:10:18.355
that's where you have to
work on your strengths.

00:10:18.355 --> 00:10:21.742
If I'm a two at something,
I'm very weak in a skill,

00:10:21.742 --> 00:10:23.308
if I worked hard I could
only become a four.

00:10:23.308 --> 00:10:24.554
I'd still be below average.

00:10:24.554 --> 00:10:26.016
That's what I tell people,

00:10:26.016 --> 00:10:26.982
you've got to ask yourself,

00:10:26.982 --> 00:10:28.109
is it going to give me the return.

00:10:28.109 --> 00:10:30.288
So, in the area of strengths,

00:10:30.288 --> 00:10:31.749
you've got to pour yourself into

00:10:31.749 --> 00:10:33.252
the things that you're already good at

00:10:33.252 --> 00:10:35.948
because that'll really set
you apart from average.

00:10:35.948 --> 00:10:40.162
But in areas of choices,
go for those weaknesses.

00:10:40.162 --> 00:10:41.582
Because don't you know people

00:10:41.582 --> 00:10:43.822
that are highly skills but
they have a lousy attitude

00:10:43.822 --> 00:10:47.160
or they're not self-disciplined.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.365
They're never going to get anywhere.

00:10:48.365 --> 00:10:50.052
And they're never going to get anywhere

00:10:50.052 --> 00:10:53.712
so in areas of choices,
work on your weaknesses.

00:10:53.712 --> 00:10:56.375
But in areas of strengths and skills,

00:10:56.375 --> 00:10:57.640
just work on your strengths.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.267
Paul Harvey said that you can tell

00:10:59.267 --> 00:11:01.554
you're on the road the success

00:11:01.554 --> 00:11:04.445
because it's uphill all the way.

00:11:04.445 --> 00:11:06.841
And I'm here to tell you,

00:11:06.841 --> 00:11:09.873
this something for nothing
entitlement mindset

00:11:09.873 --> 00:11:11.717
that invades so many people's lives

00:11:11.717 --> 00:11:13.692
is a bunch of garbage.

00:11:13.692 --> 00:11:18.418
I can promise you, nothing
that I've ever had worthwhile

00:11:18.418 --> 00:11:20.880
did I have just kind of fall into my lap,

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.865
like, oh my goodness, I won the lottery.

00:11:22.865 --> 00:11:24.966
I know once in a while
people win the lottery.

00:11:24.966 --> 00:11:26.555
But basically, the people
that win the lottery,

00:11:26.555 --> 00:11:28.797
half of them lose their
money within three years,

00:11:28.797 --> 00:11:29.667
the money they won.

00:11:29.667 --> 00:11:31.130
And it's because they got money,

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.687
but they never changed
the way they thought.

00:11:33.687 --> 00:11:34.520
And can I tell you something?

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:35.839
If you have all the assets of the world

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:37.316
but you don't change the way you think,

00:11:37.316 --> 00:11:39.615
you'll lose all the assets of the world.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:40.761
And what I've discovered

00:11:40.761 --> 00:11:43.218
in this whole process of trade-offs is,

00:11:43.218 --> 00:11:45.603
that every step of the way of my journey,

00:11:45.603 --> 00:11:47.404
I have to trade something off.

00:11:47.404 --> 00:11:49.782
And for you that are young,

00:11:49.782 --> 00:11:51.283
the good news is your trade-offs

00:11:51.283 --> 00:11:53.501
will never be easier
than when you're young.

00:11:53.501 --> 00:11:55.635
I remember when Marg and I started out,

00:11:55.635 --> 00:11:57.498
I think, man, I'd give up everything.

00:11:57.498 --> 00:11:59.705
I was a pastor to go in the Ministry,

00:11:59.705 --> 00:12:01.904
I gave up everything, and
then I started smiling.

00:12:01.904 --> 00:12:03.773
I thought, sure I gave up everything.

00:12:03.773 --> 00:12:05.993
I didn't have anything to give up.

00:12:05.993 --> 00:12:07.303
I was kid, you know what I mean?

00:12:07.303 --> 00:12:11.007
I had a 1964 Ford Falcon
and a U-Haul trailer,

00:12:11.007 --> 00:12:13.420
and five pieces of
furniture and life was grand

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:14.820
and off we went.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.263
Here's what I've discovered, Ken,

00:12:16.263 --> 00:12:17.627
and I think I want the audience,

00:12:17.627 --> 00:12:19.290
I want everyone watching around the world

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:22.689
to know today, this is very simple.

00:12:22.689 --> 00:12:24.060
The more successful you become,

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.357
the higher the trade-offs are.

00:12:26.357 --> 00:12:28.622
You see, we make a mistake
thinking it gets easier.

00:12:28.622 --> 00:12:31.025
It doesn't get easier, it gets harder.

00:12:31.025 --> 00:12:32.077
And the more you have,

00:12:32.077 --> 00:12:33.187
every time you go back and say,

00:12:33.187 --> 00:12:35.351
"am I willing to risk that again,"

00:12:35.351 --> 00:12:36.281
the more realize, wow,

00:12:36.281 --> 00:12:38.281
I'm not sure I am willing to risk this.

00:12:38.281 --> 00:12:39.537
And what I've learned is,

00:12:39.537 --> 00:12:42.742
the moment I stopped making trade-offs

00:12:42.742 --> 00:12:45.022
is the moment that I plateau.

00:12:45.022 --> 00:12:48.202
And life is continually
filled with trade-offs.

00:12:48.202 --> 00:12:50.090
In your life and in my life.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:51.574
My expression that I use all the time,

00:12:51.574 --> 00:12:52.869
Ken, you've heard me say this,

00:12:52.869 --> 00:12:55.903
you have to give up to go up.

00:12:55.903 --> 00:12:56.812
When somebody comes and says,

00:12:56.812 --> 00:12:57.645
"John I want to be a leader,"

00:12:57.645 --> 00:12:59.205
I always ask them the question why.

00:12:59.205 --> 00:13:01.125
Why do you want to be a leader?

00:13:01.125 --> 00:13:03.097
Do you want to be a leader
because you want a corner office?

00:13:03.097 --> 00:13:04.112
Do you want to be a leader because

00:13:04.112 --> 00:13:04.945
you like to be in control?

00:13:04.945 --> 00:13:06.264
Do you want to be a
leader because you love

00:13:06.264 --> 00:13:07.802
to be in the front of the pack?

00:13:07.802 --> 00:13:08.635
Do you want to be a leader because

00:13:08.635 --> 00:13:11.929
you have a good parking place?

00:13:11.929 --> 00:13:14.356
Why do you want to be a leader?

00:13:14.356 --> 00:13:15.582
Because leadership isn't easy.

00:13:15.582 --> 00:13:17.488
Leadership, a lot of times, isn't fun.

00:13:17.488 --> 00:13:21.321
Leadership, a lot of
times, is kind of lonely.

00:13:22.931 --> 00:13:26.140
There's only one reason to be a leader.

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.566
And that is to add value to people.

00:13:28.566 --> 00:13:30.845
And you and I will only
add value to people

00:13:30.845 --> 00:13:32.429
if we truly value them.

00:13:32.429 --> 00:13:34.134
I have a non-profit organization, EQUIP,

00:13:34.134 --> 00:13:35.901
that's trained almost two million leaders

00:13:35.901 --> 00:13:38.237
internationally around the
world, and I kid you not,

00:13:38.237 --> 00:13:40.278
as I go to developing
countries twice a year,

00:13:40.278 --> 00:13:41.797
and I travel internationally,

00:13:41.797 --> 00:13:43.443
and I sit down, sometimes
I have the privilege

00:13:43.443 --> 00:13:46.009
to be in the office of the
presidents of countries.

00:13:46.009 --> 00:13:48.326
And as I watch these developing countries,

00:13:48.326 --> 00:13:50.524
the thing that is the
cardinal sin among leadership

00:13:50.524 --> 00:13:52.892
so much is that they have
leaders of their countries

00:13:52.892 --> 00:13:56.102
that truly don't value the people.

00:13:56.102 --> 00:13:57.912
And if you and I don't value people,

00:13:57.912 --> 00:13:59.829
we will devalue people.

00:14:03.334 --> 00:14:06.547
Number two, if you want
to add value to people

00:14:06.547 --> 00:14:09.880
you have to make yourself more valuable.

00:14:10.831 --> 00:14:12.548
You just have to get better.

00:14:12.548 --> 00:14:14.388
You have to keep growing,
you have to keep learning,

00:14:14.388 --> 00:14:16.680
you have to keep developing.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.013
Why?
Very simply.

00:14:19.112 --> 00:14:19.945
If you're leading the pack,

00:14:19.945 --> 00:14:22.162
you have to be able to give what you have.

00:14:22.162 --> 00:14:23.594
And you can't give what you don't have.

00:14:23.594 --> 00:14:26.507
And so therefore, you've got
to put a lot of good stuff in

00:14:26.507 --> 00:14:28.290
so you can pass it on to others

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.328
and share with them and add value to them.

00:14:31.328 --> 00:14:34.995
Thirdly, if you want
to add value to people,

00:14:35.847 --> 00:14:40.214
you have to know and relate
to what other people value.

00:14:40.214 --> 00:14:41.938
You and I have to walk
slowly through the crowd.

00:14:41.938 --> 00:14:45.672
We have to listen, we have to care.

00:14:45.672 --> 00:14:47.954
You see, great leaders are,
first of all, listeners,

00:14:47.954 --> 00:14:52.221
and then they're learners,
and then they're leaders.

00:14:52.221 --> 00:14:54.413
They really do take their
cue from the people.

00:14:54.413 --> 00:14:57.250
They understand the key of leadership

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.041
is connecting with the
people that you lead,

00:14:59.041 --> 00:15:01.023
and the only way that
you and I ever connect

00:15:01.023 --> 00:15:03.523
is by caring enough to listen.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:09.926
Number four is very important to me.

00:15:09.926 --> 00:15:12.998
When I'm speaking in
the business community,

00:15:12.998 --> 00:15:15.052
I always share with them that for them

00:15:15.052 --> 00:15:16.793
there are three ways
to add value to people.

00:15:16.793 --> 00:15:19.130
But for me there are four.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.676
And I tell them I'll give them the three,

00:15:21.676 --> 00:15:23.459
so I'll spend maybe an hour doing

00:15:23.459 --> 00:15:25.681
those three things I just shared with you,

00:15:25.681 --> 00:15:27.912
talking about how to add value to people.

00:15:27.912 --> 00:15:29.718
And then only when I'm
done with the three,

00:15:29.718 --> 00:15:30.651
somebody will raise their hand and say,

00:15:30.651 --> 00:15:32.452
"John, you said that
for you there was four.

00:15:32.452 --> 00:15:33.973
"But you said there's only three for us."

00:15:33.973 --> 00:15:35.754
I said, "yes, there's nothing personal,

00:15:35.754 --> 00:15:37.821
"but this is about my faith,

00:15:37.821 --> 00:15:40.325
"and so I don't really
need to share this with you

00:15:40.325 --> 00:15:41.277
"because I'm trying to help you

00:15:41.277 --> 00:15:43.689
"and I don't want to cross
boundaries with you."

00:15:43.689 --> 00:15:46.343
And everyone will say, "Well,
John, tell us what yours is."

00:15:46.343 --> 00:15:47.799
And I'll say, "okay, you can hear mine,

00:15:47.799 --> 00:15:50.224
"but don't write it down."

00:15:50.224 --> 00:15:52.915
(audience laughs)

00:15:52.915 --> 00:15:54.915
"In fact, don't listen."

00:15:56.403 --> 00:15:58.695
Because number four for me is,

00:15:58.695 --> 00:16:02.028
if I really want to add value to people,

00:16:03.274 --> 00:16:07.338
I have to do the things that God values.

00:16:07.338 --> 00:16:10.791
You see, the greatest motivational
principle in the world,

00:16:10.791 --> 00:16:12.255
the greatest motivational
principle in the world

00:16:12.255 --> 00:16:15.588
is that people do what people see.

00:16:15.588 --> 00:16:17.838
And too many travel agents,

00:16:18.812 --> 00:16:21.921
too many travel agents, they're like,

00:16:21.921 --> 00:16:24.722
or too many leader are like travel agents.

00:16:24.722 --> 00:16:28.336
They're sending people where
they've never been themselves.

00:16:28.336 --> 00:16:29.290
And you want to be a tour guide.

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:30.981
You want to take people with you.

00:16:30.981 --> 00:16:32.481
You want to say this is
the area where I've been,

00:16:32.481 --> 00:16:33.365
this is the area where I live,

00:16:33.365 --> 00:16:34.349
this is the area where I lead,

00:16:34.349 --> 00:16:35.927
come along and follow me.

00:16:35.927 --> 00:16:37.159
And at level number three,

00:16:37.159 --> 00:16:39.590
your leadership begins
to gain credibility,

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:43.772
because now you are fleshing
out for the people around you,

00:16:43.772 --> 00:16:46.926
and you are modeling for them
things that they want to see.

00:16:46.926 --> 00:16:49.218
And you are starting to produce.

00:16:49.218 --> 00:16:53.385
- How would you tell us to
develop a habit of curiosity?

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:56.025
- Well, you know what?

00:16:56.025 --> 00:16:57.599
People do look at me
and see me as creative

00:16:57.599 --> 00:16:59.014
because I've written so many books.

00:16:59.014 --> 00:17:00.849
And I am curious.

00:17:00.849 --> 00:17:02.277
But when I started off in college,

00:17:02.277 --> 00:17:05.353
in my freshman psych class,

00:17:05.353 --> 00:17:09.520
they gave us a test for each
student to take on creativity.

00:17:10.511 --> 00:17:12.940
And there were 17 in that
class, I can remember it well.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:16.370
And I was at the bottom of
the class on creativity.

00:17:16.370 --> 00:17:20.083
I mean, I was like number 17 out of 17.

00:17:20.083 --> 00:17:21.278
The teacher posted the scores,

00:17:21.278 --> 00:17:23.528
and so I saw how bad I was.

00:17:24.384 --> 00:17:25.637
And then I really felt bad.

00:17:25.637 --> 00:17:27.392
Not only because I wasn't creative,

00:17:27.392 --> 00:17:28.777
but I was going to go into pastor,

00:17:28.777 --> 00:17:29.683
I was going to go into ministry,

00:17:29.683 --> 00:17:31.822
I was going to teach, I
was going to do sermons.

00:17:31.822 --> 00:17:34.201
And then I got to thinking to myself,

00:17:34.201 --> 00:17:36.607
I'm going to be another boring preacher.

00:17:36.607 --> 00:17:37.769
(audience laughs)

00:17:37.769 --> 00:17:38.717
That just gripped me.

00:17:38.717 --> 00:17:39.841
I thought, this is going to be awful.

00:17:39.841 --> 00:17:41.455
I'm going to bore people all my life,

00:17:41.455 --> 00:17:42.288
if you know what I mean.

00:17:42.288 --> 00:17:44.039
And then I'm going to ask them
to pay while they hear me.

00:17:44.039 --> 00:17:46.320
And this is an awful deal.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:48.776
And so two things I did.

00:17:48.776 --> 00:17:49.658
And I talked about it in the book.

00:17:49.658 --> 00:17:51.258
One is, I starting filing.

00:17:51.258 --> 00:17:52.634
I started taking thoughts and quotes.

00:17:52.634 --> 00:17:53.940
And the reason I started doing that is

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.433
I thought, if I'm not going
to be good at what I say,

00:17:56.433 --> 00:17:59.110
I need to quote somebody that is good.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:00.457
And so I started filing things

00:18:00.457 --> 00:18:01.604
that other people would say,

00:18:01.604 --> 00:18:03.714
so I'd go around and I'd
say, "well, Ken said,"

00:18:03.714 --> 00:18:04.547
you know what I mean?

00:18:04.547 --> 00:18:06.240
And Joe said, and Susie said.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:07.384
And so then at least I would be

00:18:07.384 --> 00:18:08.386
a little bit more interesting.

00:18:08.386 --> 00:18:10.722
And that's how it started.

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:13.887
But the big breakthrough I had was,

00:18:13.887 --> 00:18:15.266
and this will help everyone.

00:18:15.266 --> 00:18:16.866
How many would like to be more creative?

00:18:16.866 --> 00:18:18.294
Let me see your hands.

00:18:18.294 --> 00:18:19.617
How many of you would like to

00:18:19.617 --> 00:18:22.524
just absolutely have an imagination

00:18:22.524 --> 00:18:25.110
that just took you to a new level?

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.386
How many already have that
because you're on drugs?

00:18:27.386 --> 00:18:30.365
(audience laughs)

00:18:30.365 --> 00:18:32.247
Here's the key, this'll work for you.

00:18:32.247 --> 00:18:35.755
I promise it will work for
you because it worked for me.

00:18:35.755 --> 00:18:37.901
I made a major change in my early 30s

00:18:37.901 --> 00:18:39.859
from just having answers
and being a leader

00:18:39.859 --> 00:18:42.065
that just had to cast vision,

00:18:42.065 --> 00:18:45.064
show everybody what I was
doing and where we were going,

00:18:45.064 --> 00:18:48.335
and I started asking
intentional questions.

00:18:48.335 --> 00:18:51.451
And I became a person, I
ask questions all the time.

00:18:51.451 --> 00:18:53.935
Every day I ask questions.

00:18:53.935 --> 00:18:57.240
And I have found, just asking questions

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:00.693
will take you on exiting
journeys more than anything else.

00:19:00.693 --> 00:19:02.071
You excel in that, Ken.

00:19:02.071 --> 00:19:03.037
I've watched you over the years.

00:19:03.037 --> 00:19:03.870
You do excel, really, in that.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:06.921
But for my early years, I
didn't ask enough questions,

00:19:06.921 --> 00:19:09.202
and so therefore, I
didn't get enough ideas

00:19:09.202 --> 00:19:10.141
and thoughts from others.

00:19:10.141 --> 00:19:11.768
So when people say, "I
want to be more creative,

00:19:11.768 --> 00:19:12.601
"what's step number one?"

00:19:12.601 --> 00:19:14.528
Step number one is ask more questions.

00:19:14.528 --> 00:19:16.470
Just go to people and
start asking questions.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.617
It's a amazing what you'll learn.

00:19:18.617 --> 00:19:22.614
In Switzerland, there are some mountains

00:19:22.614 --> 00:19:26.771
that can be climbed by
people like you and me.

00:19:26.771 --> 00:19:28.109
We got to get a little
bit better condition,

00:19:28.109 --> 00:19:30.288
but it's not like the
mountain climber mountains

00:19:30.288 --> 00:19:33.106
that would be way beyond our skill level.

00:19:33.106 --> 00:19:34.818
But some of these mountains

00:19:34.818 --> 00:19:35.908
that can be climbed by you and me,

00:19:35.908 --> 00:19:37.610
it takes a day to get to the top,

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:41.474
and then you camp up there,
and you come down the next day.

00:19:41.474 --> 00:19:43.741
And on many of these places in Switzerland

00:19:43.741 --> 00:19:45.673
where you can do that,

00:19:45.673 --> 00:19:46.974
about halfway up the mountain

00:19:46.974 --> 00:19:48.455
is what they all the halfway house.

00:19:48.455 --> 00:19:50.962
Halfway house, halfway up the mountain.

00:19:50.962 --> 00:19:53.657
So a group of people starts off
with a guide in the morning,

00:19:53.657 --> 00:19:56.075
and about noon, you're kind of tired.

00:19:56.075 --> 00:19:58.197
You've been walking now
for four or five hours,

00:19:58.197 --> 00:20:00.403
and here comes, you're coming
up on the halfway house,

00:20:00.403 --> 00:20:03.772
and it is just a wonderful sight.

00:20:03.772 --> 00:20:06.053
Because as soon as you
enter the halfway house,

00:20:06.053 --> 00:20:07.272
you can smell the food,

00:20:07.272 --> 00:20:09.328
and they're getting
ready for a good lunch,

00:20:09.328 --> 00:20:10.362
and you're sitting down,

00:20:10.362 --> 00:20:12.090
there's a fire in the fireplace,

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.451
and there's nice soft chairs,

00:20:14.451 --> 00:20:17.610
you can just rest your
body and refresh your body.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.663
And so the people go
into the halfway house,

00:20:19.663 --> 00:20:21.754
they eat and laugh and enjoy,

00:20:21.754 --> 00:20:24.978
and just really, really
just kind of take in

00:20:24.978 --> 00:20:28.145
that wonderful, refreshing atmosphere.

00:20:29.083 --> 00:20:31.072
About an hour and a 1/2 after
being in the halfway house,

00:20:31.072 --> 00:20:31.940
the guide gets up and says,

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:33.630
"well, gang, it's time
to put on your gear.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:37.829
"We've got a hike, we've
got get on up to the top."

00:20:37.829 --> 00:20:39.079
This is a fact.

00:20:40.204 --> 00:20:45.004
About half the people decide
not to go on up to the top.

00:20:45.004 --> 00:20:46.252
They're sitting around the table,

00:20:46.252 --> 00:20:48.582
and they just say, "you
know what, go on up.

00:20:48.582 --> 00:20:49.869
"You're going to be
coming by here tomorrow.

00:20:49.869 --> 00:20:51.193
"You're going to be having
lunch here again tomorrow.

00:20:51.193 --> 00:20:52.469
"We're just going to hang here."

00:20:52.469 --> 00:20:54.810
And so the people that
want to get to the top,

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:56.913
they put their stuff on,
and they kind of leave,

00:20:56.913 --> 00:20:58.289
and the rest of the people stay behind,

00:20:58.289 --> 00:21:00.186
they eat a little bit more food,

00:21:00.186 --> 00:21:02.148
get around the piano, sing some songs,

00:21:02.148 --> 00:21:04.491
get around the fireplace, enjoy the fire,

00:21:04.491 --> 00:21:06.756
warm themselves by the fire.

00:21:06.756 --> 00:21:09.238
Play games all afternoon,
kind of enjoy themselves.

00:21:09.238 --> 00:21:12.573
But about 4:30, quarter
to five in the afternoon,

00:21:12.573 --> 00:21:15.073
without anybody saying a word,

00:21:16.324 --> 00:21:19.799
the people who stayed
in the halfway house,

00:21:19.799 --> 00:21:22.937
they slowly go over to
where the window is,

00:21:22.937 --> 00:21:24.187
the big window,

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:26.614
and they start looking outside,

00:21:26.614 --> 00:21:29.178
and they start looking up
at the top of that mountain.

00:21:29.178 --> 00:21:32.036
And as they look at the
top of that mountain,

00:21:32.036 --> 00:21:34.457
they see their friends.

00:21:34.457 --> 00:21:36.401
Their friends are now
getting their tents out,

00:21:36.401 --> 00:21:38.867
and they're getting ready
to prepare for the night,

00:21:38.867 --> 00:21:41.390
and it becomes very quiet.

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:42.390
Very somber.

00:21:43.462 --> 00:21:45.774
Because everybody in the
halfway house realizes

00:21:45.774 --> 00:21:47.857
the mistake they've made.

00:21:51.441 --> 00:21:54.436
They've sold their
mission for a little bit

00:21:54.436 --> 00:21:57.300
of comfort for a few hours.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.721
Next morning, down come their friends.

00:21:59.721 --> 00:22:02.220
And by noon, they come
into the halfway house.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.050
And when they come in, they are laughing,

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:05.476
they're high-fiving,

00:22:05.476 --> 00:22:06.853
they're talking about what they've seen,

00:22:06.853 --> 00:22:08.348
where they've been, experiences,

00:22:08.348 --> 00:22:10.847
the fact that it was almost
impossible to get up there.

00:22:10.847 --> 00:22:12.274
In fact, they had to help
each other get up there.

00:22:12.274 --> 00:22:14.486
And how they joined teams and held hands,

00:22:14.486 --> 00:22:16.282
and they're just telling
all of these stories,

00:22:16.282 --> 00:22:18.145
and talk about the pictures they've taken,

00:22:18.145 --> 00:22:19.423
and how they got their group picture

00:22:19.423 --> 00:22:20.965
up there with the flag at the top.

00:22:20.965 --> 00:22:23.882
And the people who remained behind,

00:22:25.623 --> 00:22:27.965
they don't say much.

00:22:27.965 --> 00:22:30.132
They just eat their lunch,

00:22:31.304 --> 00:22:32.749
and then follow the rest of them down

00:22:32.749 --> 00:22:35.375
to the bottom of the hill

00:22:35.375 --> 00:22:38.556
where their friends are waiting for them.

00:22:38.556 --> 00:22:42.515
Those who paid the price
and went to the top,

00:22:42.515 --> 00:22:47.300
who understood now is the
time to reach the mission,

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:50.633
they hug and tell stories and celebrate.

00:22:51.806 --> 00:22:54.053
And the others, they just kind of slip off

00:22:54.053 --> 00:22:56.407
and get in their car and go home.

00:22:56.407 --> 00:22:59.141
Because they realize they're never going

00:22:59.141 --> 00:23:00.934
to be what they wanted to be.

00:23:00.934 --> 00:23:02.861
They're never going to see
what they wanted to see.

00:23:02.861 --> 00:23:06.180
They're never going to do
what they wanted to do.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.168
Because they failed to realize,

00:23:08.168 --> 00:23:10.536
when they were at the halfway house,

00:23:10.536 --> 00:23:13.703
now's the time to rest and then go on.

00:23:15.457 --> 00:23:18.374
Connecting increases your influence

00:23:19.447 --> 00:23:21.756
in every situation.

00:23:21.756 --> 00:23:23.483
For the foundation of the teaching today,

00:23:23.483 --> 00:23:25.013
I want to make sure that we really have

00:23:25.013 --> 00:23:28.724
a grasp of why connecting
with people is so vital.

00:23:28.724 --> 00:23:30.876
So if you'll go to the very
first sentence in your notes,

00:23:30.876 --> 00:23:33.573
the number one criteria for
advancement and promotion

00:23:33.573 --> 00:23:38.390
for professionals is an ability
to communicate effectively.

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:40.048
That I do believe.

00:23:40.048 --> 00:23:42.365
I think the ability to
connect and communicate

00:23:42.365 --> 00:23:44.146
is the number one criteria for success

00:23:44.146 --> 00:23:46.130
with your family, with your marriage,

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.369
with your children, with your friends,

00:23:48.369 --> 00:23:52.675
in the workplace, the
marketplace, in the community.

00:23:52.675 --> 00:23:54.640
If you have the ability
to connect with people,

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:57.400
if you can communicate and connect,

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:59.492
not just communicate,

00:23:59.492 --> 00:24:01.697
it takes you and literally
gives you an eye,

00:24:01.697 --> 00:24:03.512
a decided edge over others in

00:24:03.512 --> 00:24:06.332
the advancement of what
we're trying to accomplish.

00:24:06.332 --> 00:24:08.454
For example, in your notes,

00:24:08.454 --> 00:24:11.958
presidential historian Robert Dallek says

00:24:11.958 --> 00:24:15.735
that the successful
presidents exhibit five skills

00:24:15.735 --> 00:24:17.063
and qualities that enable them

00:24:17.063 --> 00:24:20.489
to achieve things that others don't.

00:24:20.489 --> 00:24:23.693
So he's talking about leaders
and presidents and people.

00:24:23.693 --> 00:24:25.327
What makes them achieve
better than others?

00:24:25.327 --> 00:24:26.725
Here are the five things they do well.

00:24:26.725 --> 00:24:29.282
Number one is vision.

00:24:29.282 --> 00:24:31.199
Number two, pragmatism.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.770
Number three, consensus-building.

00:24:37.216 --> 00:24:40.981
The ability to put together
a team, get consensus.

00:24:40.981 --> 00:24:42.814
Number four, charisma.

00:24:45.099 --> 00:24:47.849
And number five, trustworthiness.

00:24:49.041 --> 00:24:51.690
Being a trustworthy kind of a person.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.471
Now, what is interesting about these five,

00:24:55.471 --> 00:24:58.791
these five things that set
the better presidents apart

00:24:58.791 --> 00:24:59.920
from maybe the average ones,

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:03.087
four of the five deal with connecting.

00:25:04.484 --> 00:25:07.745
Vision is a definitely connecting skill.

00:25:07.745 --> 00:25:09.920
The ability to cast a future vision.

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:11.973
Consensus-building, getting
people to work together.

00:25:11.973 --> 00:25:13.687
You have to be able to connect to do that.

00:25:13.687 --> 00:25:14.806
Charisma, of course.

00:25:14.806 --> 00:25:17.220
That's why people follow
the, quote, the pied piper.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:18.180
And trustworthy.

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:21.214
Four of the five of the skills needed

00:25:21.214 --> 00:25:23.256
to be successful as a leader had

00:25:23.256 --> 00:25:26.149
a lot to do with connecting.

00:25:26.149 --> 00:25:27.408
So let me define connecting,

00:25:27.408 --> 00:25:30.152
because that's what
this book is all about.

00:25:30.152 --> 00:25:34.235
Connecting is the ability
to identify with people

00:25:35.216 --> 00:25:37.553
and relate to them in such a way

00:25:37.553 --> 00:25:41.410
that it increases our influence with them.

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:43.303
In other words, we can identify with them

00:25:43.303 --> 00:25:45.887
until they say, "he or she understands

00:25:45.887 --> 00:25:47.973
"exactly what I know and
what I'm going through."

00:25:47.973 --> 00:25:49.922
and we can relate with them until we,

00:25:49.922 --> 00:25:51.960
because of that ability,

00:25:51.960 --> 00:25:54.290
we begin to increase
our influence with them.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:56.101
And of course, what do I
teach about leadership?

00:25:56.101 --> 00:25:59.520
Very simple, leadership is influence.

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:01.385
I think success, first of all,

00:26:01.385 --> 00:26:03.885
is knowing my purpose in life.

00:26:05.584 --> 00:26:08.185
As I have watched and
observed successful people,

00:26:08.185 --> 00:26:10.904
what I have discovered about them is,

00:26:10.904 --> 00:26:15.311
they really have figured
out why they're here.

00:26:15.311 --> 00:26:18.468
They really do have their act together.

00:26:18.468 --> 00:26:21.218
And knowing their purpose in life

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:24.607
is a stability for them.

00:26:24.607 --> 00:26:26.723
So that when everyone else
is rocking and rolling,

00:26:26.723 --> 00:26:29.286
and things are a little unsteady,

00:26:29.286 --> 00:26:30.991
and people are leaving the ship,

00:26:30.991 --> 00:26:34.272
people are kind of
abandoning their causes,

00:26:34.272 --> 00:26:37.350
these people, they hold steady
right throughout the storm.

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:39.525
Because they have a true north star.

00:26:39.525 --> 00:26:41.211
They truly are focused.

00:26:41.211 --> 00:26:44.390
It becomes an anchor in their life

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:45.893
that just holds them steady.

00:26:45.893 --> 00:26:48.393
And the anchor is a confidence

00:26:51.175 --> 00:26:54.008
based upon a knowledge of purpose.

00:26:58.515 --> 00:26:59.832
Someone said that there are

00:26:59.832 --> 00:27:02.665
two great days in a person's life.

00:27:04.471 --> 00:27:07.475
The day that they are born,

00:27:07.475 --> 00:27:09.975
and the day they discover why.

00:27:10.814 --> 00:27:14.534
I'm here to tell you,
highly successful people

00:27:14.534 --> 00:27:16.451
they've discovered why.

00:27:18.632 --> 00:27:19.580
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:22.332
I made this video because
John Fontana asked me to.

00:27:22.332 --> 00:27:23.504
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:27:23.504 --> 00:27:24.979
that you want me to profile next,

00:27:24.979 --> 00:27:27.295
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:27:27.295 --> 00:27:28.917
I also love to know
which of John Maxwell's

00:27:28.917 --> 00:27:31.379
top 10 rules had the biggest
impact on you and why.

00:27:31.379 --> 00:27:34.070
Leave it in the comments, and
I will join in the discussion.

00:27:34.070 --> 00:27:35.323
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:27:35.323 --> 00:27:36.352
to Rhonda Young.

00:27:36.352 --> 00:27:38.032
Rhonda, thank you so
much for buying my book.

00:27:38.032 --> 00:27:39.149
It means a lot to me.

00:27:39.149 --> 00:27:39.982
For those of you watching,

00:27:39.982 --> 00:27:42.601
if you want your chance at a
shout-out in a future video,

00:27:42.601 --> 00:27:44.176
make sure to pick up a copy of the book,

00:27:44.176 --> 00:27:45.633
and then email in your receipt

00:27:45.633 --> 00:27:47.149
so I can keep track of you

00:27:47.149 --> 00:27:48.320
and give you some acknowledgement.

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:49.962
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:27:49.962 --> 00:27:52.324
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is,

00:27:52.324 --> 00:27:54.057
and I'll see you soon.

00:27:54.057 --> 00:27:56.724
(thunder roars)

00:28:00.096 --> 00:28:03.934
- I've never met a person
who was not successful

00:28:03.934 --> 00:28:04.954
that didn't have a great amount

00:28:04.954 --> 00:28:07.643
of self-discipline within their life.

00:28:07.643 --> 00:28:11.089
Self-discipline and being able to perform,

00:28:11.089 --> 00:28:14.104
and being able to keep
your life on schedule

00:28:14.104 --> 00:28:15.563
and being able to keep commitments

00:28:15.563 --> 00:28:19.730
and promises and meet deadlines
is essential to success.

00:28:20.978 --> 00:28:24.262
None of us, none of us
can afford to have a life

00:28:24.262 --> 00:28:27.071
that is controlled by someone else,

00:28:27.071 --> 00:28:31.156
or a life that is basically
controlled by our emotions.

00:28:31.156 --> 00:28:34.420
I learned many years ago that
there are two kinds of people.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:36.393
There's the type of person who says,

00:28:36.393 --> 00:28:39.655
"I'm going to wait till I
feel like it before I do it."

00:28:39.655 --> 00:28:40.718
And then there's the person who says,

00:28:40.718 --> 00:28:43.471
"I've got to do it so
that I feel like it."

00:28:43.471 --> 00:28:45.829
One will never get anything done

00:28:45.829 --> 00:28:49.153
because they're still waiting
to feel the moment to move,

00:28:49.153 --> 00:28:51.841
and the other person
says, "no, I need I move,

00:28:51.841 --> 00:28:54.316
"and then I will begin
to feel the moment."

00:28:54.316 --> 00:28:57.090
Assigning yourself discipline is essential

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:59.499
in your life and in my life

00:28:59.499 --> 00:29:01.712
if we're going to get things done.

00:29:01.712 --> 00:29:04.395
So I have a challenge for
every one of you this weekend.

00:29:04.395 --> 00:29:06.177
The challenge is simple.

00:29:06.177 --> 00:29:07.935
As you go through your weekend,

00:29:07.935 --> 00:29:09.843
and sometimes it's kind of, hopefully,

00:29:09.843 --> 00:29:12.176
an easier time of your week,

00:29:13.035 --> 00:29:17.883
ask yourself, am I practicing
self-discipline in my life?

00:29:17.883 --> 00:29:19.800
Am I doing the things that I should do

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.165
because I need to do them,

00:29:21.165 --> 00:29:25.299
or am I kind of waiting
to feel the moment?

00:29:25.299 --> 00:29:29.251
Do like our friends Nike say, just do it.

00:29:29.251 --> 00:29:33.212
- How do find our
personal Kurt Kiermaiers?

00:29:33.212 --> 00:29:35.982
Somebody who can really
speak into our lives

00:29:35.982 --> 00:29:38.236
and change our trajectory?

00:29:38.236 --> 00:29:40.626
- Well, I can't take credit for it.

00:29:40.626 --> 00:29:45.075
I went to a seminar that
Kurt was teaching on success,

00:29:45.075 --> 00:29:48.075
and when he finished
teaching I went up to him

00:29:48.075 --> 00:29:49.359
and introduced myself.

00:29:49.359 --> 00:29:52.942
And I asked for an appointment with him.

00:29:52.942 --> 00:29:55.115
And he came, then the Lancaster

00:29:55.115 --> 00:29:56.952
to the Holiday Inn for breakfast,

00:29:56.952 --> 00:29:59.124
and we had that breakfast
meeting that did change my life.

00:29:59.124 --> 00:30:01.291
And then I never saw
him again from that time

00:30:01.291 --> 00:30:04.784
until my 65th birthday
when my staff brought,

00:30:04.784 --> 00:30:05.841
I never had seen him.

00:30:05.841 --> 00:30:07.391
I talked about him, and talked about,

00:30:07.391 --> 00:30:11.013
on my 65th birthday, when we
were having a party together,

00:30:11.013 --> 00:30:13.027
they brought him into the room.

00:30:13.027 --> 00:30:14.527
And I hadn't seen him for 40 years.

00:30:14.527 --> 00:30:15.400
I just wept.

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:16.388
And I thought, you know.

00:30:16.388 --> 00:30:17.559
But I think the key was,

00:30:17.559 --> 00:30:19.351
when I went to hear him,

00:30:19.351 --> 00:30:22.219
and he began to speak to my
life about being successful,

00:30:22.219 --> 00:30:25.130
I kind of broke out of a barrier

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:27.082
and I went to introduce
myself, and then I asked,

00:30:27.082 --> 00:30:30.097
could I take him to breakfast
and could I learn from him.

00:30:30.097 --> 00:30:31.033
And I think the key is,

00:30:31.033 --> 00:30:34.418
once you see somebody that is helping you,

00:30:34.418 --> 00:30:37.704
be bold and ask them, "can
I take you to breakfast."

00:30:37.704 --> 00:30:38.810
And by the way, here's a thought.

00:30:38.810 --> 00:30:40.377
Buy the breakfast, okay?

00:30:40.377 --> 00:30:41.301
(Ken and audience laugh)

00:30:41.301 --> 00:30:42.865
Just don't go and say, "hey, how about

00:30:42.865 --> 00:30:44.967
"buying breakfast for me?"

00:30:44.967 --> 00:30:47.545
But just, I took that step,

00:30:47.545 --> 00:30:48.760
and I've always been
glad I took that step,

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:50.054
and I've thought to myself, you know what?

00:30:50.054 --> 00:30:53.404
If I wouldn't have taken the step,

00:30:53.404 --> 00:30:56.117
then I probably would have
never had that relationship,

00:30:56.117 --> 00:30:57.842
and never maybe would
have asked that question,

00:30:57.842 --> 00:30:59.492
and never would have this seminar

00:30:59.492 --> 00:31:01.900
or would have written the book.

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:03.823
So I think once you see somebody

00:31:03.823 --> 00:31:05.488
that you think can really help you,

00:31:05.488 --> 00:31:07.784
take the initiative and step right out.

00:31:07.784 --> 00:31:11.065
Something magical happens when you begin

00:31:11.065 --> 00:31:13.024
to understand the most appreciable asset

00:31:13.024 --> 00:31:14.295
you have in any organization

00:31:14.295 --> 00:31:16.942
is the people of that organization.

00:31:16.942 --> 00:31:18.017
And you begin to commit yourself

00:31:18.017 --> 00:31:20.161
to develop the people
in that organization.

00:31:20.161 --> 00:31:21.772
Because how do you grow a company?

00:31:21.772 --> 00:31:25.663
You grow a company by growing people.

00:31:25.663 --> 00:31:28.086
And when you grow them, they
increase their capacity.

00:31:28.086 --> 00:31:29.685
And when they an increase their capacity,

00:31:29.685 --> 00:31:31.488
they begin to increase the capacity

00:31:31.488 --> 00:31:35.405
of what you can do and
what you can accomplish.

00:31:38.588 --> 00:31:41.261
Let me just share with you quickly

00:31:41.261 --> 00:31:45.547
three thoughts on developing
people, just very quickly.

00:31:45.547 --> 00:31:48.462
Number one is, the key
to developing leaders

00:31:48.462 --> 00:31:53.117
and the key to developing
good people is in recruitment.

00:31:53.117 --> 00:31:54.599
The better person you bring in the door,

00:31:54.599 --> 00:31:57.312
the higher odds that you
can do well with them.

00:31:57.312 --> 00:31:58.944
I remember having lunch
one time with Lou Holtz,

00:31:58.944 --> 00:31:59.777
and Lou Holtz said,

00:31:59.777 --> 00:32:02.235
"John, I've had bad players
on my football team,

00:32:02.235 --> 00:32:03.941
"and I've had good players
on my football team."

00:32:03.941 --> 00:32:05.034
And he said, "just to be honest with you,

00:32:05.034 --> 00:32:07.736
"I'm a better coach with good players."

00:32:07.736 --> 00:32:10.071
(audience laughs)

00:32:10.071 --> 00:32:12.023
Of course he is.

00:32:12.023 --> 00:32:12.856
Of course he is.

00:32:12.856 --> 00:32:13.689
Let me tell you something.

00:32:13.689 --> 00:32:17.856
80% of your success of equipping
people to be successful

00:32:19.086 --> 00:32:22.503
is in the front door on who you bring in.

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:25.305
I wish I had time to develop that for you

00:32:25.305 --> 00:32:26.707
because what happens is,

00:32:26.707 --> 00:32:28.326
unless you and I have a clear picture

00:32:28.326 --> 00:32:30.012
of what we're looking for,

00:32:30.012 --> 00:32:32.569
we don't know when we see it.

00:32:32.569 --> 00:32:34.151
Happens all the time.

00:32:34.151 --> 00:32:34.984
People come to me and say,

00:32:34.984 --> 00:32:37.331
"John, I'm looking for some
leaders for my company.

00:32:37.331 --> 00:32:38.164
"What do you suggest?"

00:32:38.164 --> 00:32:41.049
The first question I ask is
what does a leader look like?

00:32:41.049 --> 00:32:43.052
Give me the characteristics,
give the qualities.

00:32:43.052 --> 00:32:45.311
Please, please, paint a picture for me.

00:32:45.311 --> 00:32:46.463
What does that leader look like?

00:32:46.463 --> 00:32:48.002
Because you got to have that clear picture

00:32:48.002 --> 00:32:50.667
so that when you see that person you know

00:32:50.667 --> 00:32:51.758
they're the one that you want.

00:32:51.758 --> 00:32:52.591
Recruitment is key.

00:32:52.591 --> 00:32:54.745
Number two is positioning.

00:32:54.745 --> 00:32:55.639
The ability not only to bring

00:32:55.639 --> 00:32:58.538
the right person in the front door,

00:32:58.538 --> 00:32:59.778
but also put them in the right places.

00:32:59.778 --> 00:33:01.623
Collins would say get them on the bus.

00:33:01.623 --> 00:33:03.737
Get them on the right seat.

00:33:03.737 --> 00:33:06.146
And position is huge.

00:33:06.146 --> 00:33:08.767
And what I've discovered
is that successful people

00:33:08.767 --> 00:33:11.476
have always positioned themselves well.

00:33:11.476 --> 00:33:12.672
When you see a successful person,

00:33:12.672 --> 00:33:14.657
they're successful because
what they have done

00:33:14.657 --> 00:33:16.227
is they have found their strength zone

00:33:16.227 --> 00:33:17.501
and they've found their niche,

00:33:17.501 --> 00:33:19.173
and they stay right in that sweet spot.

00:33:19.173 --> 00:33:20.962
And they just work that sweet spot.

00:33:20.962 --> 00:33:22.618
You've never heard
anybody being interviewed

00:33:22.618 --> 00:33:25.015
that was successful that you
came up and your interviewed

00:33:25.015 --> 00:33:26.321
and they said, "well,
the secret of my success

00:33:26.321 --> 00:33:29.898
"is that I never discovered
what I was good at."

00:33:29.898 --> 00:33:30.949
Or, "the secret of my success is

00:33:30.949 --> 00:33:33.334
"I've just worked on my weakness."

00:33:33.334 --> 00:33:34.965
No, no, they're always in their strength.

00:33:34.965 --> 00:33:36.756
They've already discovered
what they're good at.

00:33:36.756 --> 00:33:40.898
Now, successful people have
discovered what they're good at.

00:33:40.898 --> 00:33:45.065
Successful leaders discover
what other people are good at.

00:33:46.636 --> 00:33:48.791
Successful people
position themselves well.

00:33:48.791 --> 00:33:52.948
A successful leader position
the other people well.

00:33:52.948 --> 00:33:54.968
And at level number four,
that's exactly what happened.

00:33:54.968 --> 00:33:57.704
The leader is always
looking at the people,

00:33:57.704 --> 00:33:59.371
observing, watching.

00:34:00.828 --> 00:34:03.911
Just like last week, I went
back to my first school.

00:34:03.911 --> 00:34:06.714
I graduated from Ohio Christian University

00:34:06.714 --> 00:34:09.237
in the little town of Circleville, Ohio.

00:34:09.237 --> 00:34:10.154
And it's a little school.

00:34:10.154 --> 00:34:12.769
I go back every year and I
give them a day to speak,

00:34:12.769 --> 00:34:15.615
and they get all the
proceeds, and keep the money.

00:34:15.615 --> 00:34:18.380
And so because I grew up in that town,

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:20.769
I told one of my friends
that play ball with me, Tim,

00:34:20.769 --> 00:34:22.708
I said, "Tim, let the ball players,

00:34:22.708 --> 00:34:24.814
"let the team know, let some
of my high school kids know

00:34:24.814 --> 00:34:25.810
"that I'm going to be in town,

00:34:25.810 --> 00:34:27.668
"and let's have a dinner afterwards."

00:34:27.668 --> 00:34:29.569
And we had over 50 people came together.

00:34:29.569 --> 00:34:32.891
I hadn't seen many of my high school mates

00:34:32.891 --> 00:34:37.014
since I graduated from high
school clear back in 1965.

00:34:37.014 --> 00:34:37.847
And we had a wonderful time.

00:34:37.847 --> 00:34:39.803
We laughed and talked and
told stories for two hours,

00:34:39.803 --> 00:34:42.614
and Coach Neff, our basketball
coach, he came back.

00:34:42.614 --> 00:34:44.708
I hadn't seen him for years.

00:34:44.708 --> 00:34:46.509
And when I saw him, I thought to myself,

00:34:46.509 --> 00:34:49.423
and I thought of back in those
scrimmages in basketball,

00:34:49.423 --> 00:34:51.683
because the first scrimmage every year,

00:34:51.683 --> 00:34:54.750
Coach Neff would take the first team,

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:55.729
and he'd say, "now, I'm going

00:34:55.729 --> 00:34:58.364
"to let you play the second team,

00:34:58.364 --> 00:34:59.309
"but there's one condition.

00:34:59.309 --> 00:35:02.060
"The first team is not going
to play their position."

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:04.093
And he'd take Doug Roth, who is 6'8"

00:35:04.093 --> 00:35:05.944
and put him out at point guard.

00:35:05.944 --> 00:35:08.683
I remember he stuck me
one year under center.

00:35:08.683 --> 00:35:10.558
And he put all the first team players

00:35:10.558 --> 00:35:12.591
out of position on an offense.

00:35:12.591 --> 00:35:15.336
But he let the second team
play in their position.

00:35:15.336 --> 00:35:16.753
And the second team always
beat us in the scrimmage.

00:35:16.753 --> 00:35:17.648
Why did they always beat us?

00:35:17.648 --> 00:35:18.967
Because, and then he would
stop and he would say,

00:35:18.967 --> 00:35:20.256
"let me just explain something to you.

00:35:20.256 --> 00:35:21.651
"No matter how talented you are,

00:35:21.651 --> 00:35:23.500
"if you're out of position,

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.471
"you'll never reach your potential."

00:35:25.471 --> 00:35:26.501
What was Coach Neff teaching us?

00:35:26.501 --> 00:35:28.022
He was coaching us and he was teaching us

00:35:28.022 --> 00:35:30.141
that it's key to get positionally correct.

00:35:30.141 --> 00:35:31.239
And it's also, as the leader,

00:35:31.239 --> 00:35:33.216
key to get your people
in the right position.

00:35:33.216 --> 00:35:35.620
And at level number four,
that's what happens.

00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:38.584
At level number four, you recruit well,

00:35:38.584 --> 00:35:42.167
you position well, and
then you equip well.

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:45.621
Now you take them and say, okay,

00:35:45.621 --> 00:35:48.047
I understand what their strength zone is.

00:35:48.047 --> 00:35:49.830
I understand what their gift in this is.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:52.145
Now I'm going to equip them.

00:35:52.145 --> 00:35:53.392
I'm going to come along side of them

00:35:53.392 --> 00:35:55.652
and I'm going to develop them
and I'm going to train them.

00:35:55.652 --> 00:35:57.586
And I use a very simple,

00:35:57.586 --> 00:35:58.988
for years, for many, many years,

00:35:58.988 --> 00:36:00.942
a simple five-step equipping process.

00:36:00.942 --> 00:36:02.633
It really works.

00:36:02.633 --> 00:36:04.499
Step one, I do it.

00:36:04.499 --> 00:36:05.879
It's that simple.

00:36:05.879 --> 00:36:09.212
You can't teach somebody
what you can't do yourself.

00:36:09.212 --> 00:36:10.600
We may teach what we know,

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:11.981
but we reproduce what we are.

00:36:11.981 --> 00:36:13.849
So if you're going to reproduce yourself,

00:36:13.849 --> 00:36:15.122
you got to be it yourself.

00:36:15.122 --> 00:36:17.905
So the first step is I do it.

00:36:17.905 --> 00:36:19.895
Step two is I do it and you're with me.

00:36:19.895 --> 00:36:20.728
I take you with me.

00:36:20.728 --> 00:36:22.036
Now we're going to spend today together,

00:36:22.036 --> 00:36:22.869
I'm going to be your mentor,

00:36:22.869 --> 00:36:24.148
I'm going to be your coach,

00:36:24.148 --> 00:36:25.769
you're going to watch me,
you're going to observe me,

00:36:25.769 --> 00:36:27.578
you're going to see me
in different situations,

00:36:27.578 --> 00:36:29.989
we're going to make sure that it works.

00:36:29.989 --> 00:36:31.941
You're going to be able
to ask me questions.

00:36:31.941 --> 00:36:33.805
Step three is, now we turn it.

00:36:33.805 --> 00:36:35.577
And I hand the ball off to you.

00:36:35.577 --> 00:36:38.077
Now, in step three, you do it.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:41.382
You do it and I'm with you.

00:36:41.382 --> 00:36:43.132
Now I'm watching you.

00:36:43.971 --> 00:36:48.514
I'm tweaking you and I'm
helping you to get better.

00:36:48.514 --> 00:36:51.181
I'm just really fine-tuning you.

00:36:54.016 --> 00:36:56.152
Step four is you do it.

00:36:56.152 --> 00:36:57.637
You just do it.

00:36:57.637 --> 00:36:59.231
You don't need me anymore,
you know how to do it.

00:36:59.231 --> 00:37:01.214
You do it, and you do it well.

00:37:01.214 --> 00:37:02.979
And many times, people
think that equipping stops

00:37:02.979 --> 00:37:04.500
at step four, but it doesn't.

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:06.519
There's one more step
that's absolutely essential.

00:37:06.519 --> 00:37:10.436
Step five is you do it
and somebody's with you.

00:37:12.239 --> 00:37:13.555
You're never really
trained to equip somebody

00:37:13.555 --> 00:37:15.716
until they can multiply themselves.

00:37:15.716 --> 00:37:17.189
That's where all compounding is.

00:37:17.189 --> 00:37:19.055
That's where all
compounding of time, money,

00:37:19.055 --> 00:37:20.660
influence, the whole process.

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:23.336
Compounding begins when
you can train somebody

00:37:23.336 --> 00:37:25.061
who has trained somebody.

00:37:25.061 --> 00:37:27.939
I know that well because 14 years ago

00:37:27.939 --> 00:37:30.237
I started a non-profit
organization called EQUIP

00:37:30.237 --> 00:37:31.945
that is now the largest
leadership organization

00:37:31.945 --> 00:37:32.778
in the world.

00:37:32.778 --> 00:37:34.950
And we trained and equipped
over three million people

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.726
in 154 countries.

00:37:36.726 --> 00:37:40.026
And the key to that is
we don't train anybody

00:37:40.026 --> 00:37:42.808
unless they make a commitment
to train somebody else.

00:37:42.808 --> 00:37:45.082
It has to continue to go on.

00:37:45.082 --> 00:37:46.935
That's level number four.

00:37:46.935 --> 00:37:48.054
It's a beautiful level to be on.

00:37:48.054 --> 00:37:51.070
It's the level where you develop people.

